{{smaller block|He hath an ''abstract'' for the remembrance of such places, and goes to them by his note.{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iv. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' In ''gram.'', an abstract term or noun.
{{smaller block|The concrete “like” has its ''abstract'' “likeness”; the concretes “father” and “son” have, or might have, the ''abstracts'' “paternity” and “filiety” or “filiation.”{{right|''J. S. Mill.''}}}}
{{smaller|'''Abstract of title''', in ''law'', an epitome or a short statement of the successive title-deeds or other evidences of ownership of an estate, and of the encumbrances thereon.—'''In the abstract''' [L. ''in abstracto''], conceived apart from matter or special circumstances; without reference to particular applications; in its general principles or meanings.}}
{{smaller block|Were all things red, the conception of colour ''in the abstract'' could not exist.{{right|''H. Spencer'', Data of Ethics, § 46.}}}}
{{smaller block|Be the system of absolute religious equality good or bad, pious or profane, ''in the abstract'', neither churchmen nor statesmen can afford to ignore the question, How will it work?{{right|''H. N. Oxenham'', Short Studies, p. 401.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' ''Abridgment, Compendium, Epitome, Abstract'', etc. See ''abridgment''.}}

